### Language and note amendment: Wave 14 LANG3: surviving audiences and diagnostic consequence

Commissioned by Charlie Cheever on 2026-10-16 for 0.4.31. This amends
§10's source-diagnostic readability fragment, LLP 3000.036.001's completeness
reach, and LLP 3000.039's diagnostic presentation. Authorization recipes and
refusal-layer text belong to RULES4; null fixture aliases belong to KIT3.

**Captured parent proof.** A server query over an ordinary online table can
prove its page's read rule from a dominating, readable point snapshot and
its surviving control paths. A held point says that its read rule held at
that point; it does not say every child is readable. For example, after an
observer branch returns, a readable observation with `held = true` and
`read <- .observer = viewer or (.teacher = viewer and .held = true)` proves
that the remaining caller is its teacher. A child page pinning that exact
observation and its captured teacher can then prove
`.teacher = viewer and exists observations[.observation, held = true]`.
The page remains exactly the authored page; no filter, audience or order is
added or removed. The ordinary meter and page frontier remain in force.

The core independently rederives this sufficient proof from the program,
including externally supplied IR; no source-only marker grants authority.
It accepts read-only query bodies, once-bound point snapshots of total
unique keys from unchanged arguments/viewer/literals, positive row rules,
at most twelve independent propositional atoms and 1,024 formula nodes. Snapshot presence
implies its actual read rule. Unknown policy components contribute no
assumption; unknown positive child branches cannot help prove the goal.
The proof accounts for both conditional paths, requirements and early
returns. Rebinding, loops, expression blocks, pair-return sinks, mutation
bodies, copied audience conventions, read-through, maintenance, field owners,
lifetimes and synced outer tables do not establish this proof. A probe may
be witnessed only by the exact captured table/index/key and required field
values. Nullable Boolean false is never inferred from not-true. The setup of a lowered short-circuit temporary confers no completed-condition
facts. Nullable snapshot ids stay null in path comparisons; a positive
exact-key witness may equate its own id only together with its presence.
Interpreter-owned `$filter`/`$project` bindings confer no snapshot evidence.
Fresh-id evaluations, reads and builtins are not symbolic comparison
identities. A reconstructed short circuit may reference only once-bound
values; a reference to its own prior value or any other assigned temporary
declines this proof. Inconsistent path facts grant no trusted scan: the
sufficient implication must also have a satisfiable antecedent. Every scan
sharing an IR site must prove the verdict. Failure retains the existing
honest incomplete answer. This server-only analysis adds no IR/wire form or
device interpretation; local device coverage and authorization are unchanged.

**Finite flow facts.** Enum equality/inequality narrows the exact binding or
snapshot field. A true disjunction (or false conjunction) unions the
alternatives; it never assumes one alternative holds. Existing surviving-
branch joins carry the fact after a return/refusal. Rebinding and loop joins
retain their existing invalidation. Sorting a complete population is a
permutation and retains its original cap/uniformity proof; slicing, joining,
filtering or transforming a population does not gain that proof.

**Immutable patch diagnostics.** A conflict key that itself proves the read
rule (for example, owner pinned to viewer) now permits the same proven-change
and fresh-clock/id diagnostics as a public readable target. Lifetime and
hidden-target exclusions remain. Unknown argument timestamps remain notes;
an owner key for another principal cannot turn its hidden conflict into a
static inequality oracle. Exact local-insert/key equality still wins before
a clock diagnostic. An immutable field projected from the immutable parent
identified by an immutable, pinned target reference is treated as an
intentional stable equality guard and does not repeat N_IMMUTABLE_PATCH.
This is note suppression, not a storage-equality claim: historical rows still
undergo runtime equality, and no parent/child invariant is inferred. A mutable
parent field or unpinned parent identity keeps the note. All remaining
N_IMMUTABLE_PATCH notes are review notes that can cost correctness.

**Proven guard notes.** Proven independent roster facts survive unrelated
writes only when neither the target nor any of its read dependencies
was written. A delete includes the schema's complete cascade/set-null write
closure. Helpers, derived maintenance and row-derived evidence retain
conservative invalidation. N_LOOKUP_HIDDEN follows the actual read
proof at an actual required point-read gate. A blind mutable update is not
a program read and does not receive N_LOOKUP_HIDDEN merely because its table
write rule admits nonreaders. A required lookup names its actual refusal code;
its note is silent when earlier guards prove that exact row readable. Rebound,
wrong or volatile keys and effectful operands do not establish that witness.
A point update counts as an earlier target
lookup attempt for N_REFERENCE_UNCHECKED, just as a nullable point read does,
including through a known helper only when every successful path performs
that lookup. Silence of that note never proves existence or successful authorization.
These notes change neither authorization nor reference integrity. A reference still validates an id, not existence.

Hidden-lookup candidates are analyzed per required-read occurrence; only surviving
advice is grouped. Named shapes and spreads are opaque effect barriers just
as function helpers are, so they cannot preserve a revoked roster witness.

**Presentation.** The default N_CONDITIONAL_COMPLETE presentation names the
query and missing pin; its checked rewrite preserves the whole intended
audience. Cursorless top-N notes group their queries under one explanation.
E_CASCADE_BOUND leads with an authored edit and “this is a product limit you
choose”; arithmetic is retained in JSON and `--verbose` details. When a
second cascading parent makes the product's honest caps impossible, the
alternative is “make one edge `restrict` or drop the column”. E_SYNC prints a
literal replacement line and names full-acquisition costs when dropping a
union horizon. A mixed positional/named-key refusal names that mix; the
cheatsheet and declaration errors identify the reserved words.

**Qualification.** Regression tests cover positive proofs and their missing-
guard, wrong-parent, rebinding, nullable, hidden-target and transformed-list
counterexamples. Run the five-crate suites, affected clients, native/device/
Lean differentials, the before/after hundred-schema survey, packed guide
qualification, wasm measurement and repository checks. Independent Muse and
fresh Codex reviews must both be READY; the workstream report binds their
artifacts, test results and exact commits. The final implementation received READY from [fresh Codex](./reviews/3000.036.002-lang3-r8.codex.md) and [Muse](./reviews/3000.036.002-lang3-r8.muse.md). [Provenance](./reviews/3000.036.002-lang3-r8.provenance.json) binds the same final implementation digests; all earlier rounds and corrections remain recorded. Their verdicts concern implementation soundness; the separate qualification receipts and any inherited check failures are recorded in the workstream report.

## 13. Wave 15 LANG4: notes builders can review

This amendment is in review until the two recorded independent reviews say
READY. It changes diagnostics and adds literal-list membership to maintained
predicates; it changes no authorization, wire, reference lifetime or sync law.
RULES5 owns authorization recipes and live-parent authority.

### 13.1 Notes and declarations

A declared continuation (`after c` or `before c`) suppresses N_NO_CURSOR.
A mandatory exact-id lookup later in the same atomic mutation also suppresses
N_REFERENCE_UNCHECKED: successful return commits the entire mutation. A lookup
on only some committing paths, in an optional loop, or through a rebound input
must not suppress it. The note describes a missing lookup, not a foreign key
proof: even an ordinary optional lookup is allowed to observe absence.
This supersedes LLP 3000.039 §7’s exclusion of later lookups.

E_QUARRY_SHAPE_JOIN names both row tables even in nested containers. A duplicate
table effect gets E_QUARRY_DUPLICATE_RULE with the first declaration’s site;
retain the first rule and usable table for recovery, avoiding invented unknown
tables. No invalid schema may execute. Maintained `x in [a,b]` lowers to the
existing typed equality predicates joined by `or` (empty list is false);
only literal-list operands accepted by the existing comparison binder qualify.
There is no new predicate serialization or interpreter operation.

N_UNREADABLE_PROBE names the total unique key covering a probe when present,
without claiming that uniqueness makes a hidden row visible. N_SYNC_ORDER
states that ordinary reads need the server and cannot provide a complete
answer offline for this order; held reads stay local and incomplete.
N_IMMUTABLE_PATCH is silent when lexical resolution proves equality to a
pinned key, including bound row fields; uncertain control flow stays a note.
N_REQUIRE_REFUSED checks each human branch independently of service branches;
a service alternative in a table rule cannot discharge a human gate’s missing
role proof. A returned query predicate whose explicit alternatives include a
branch not establishing the table’s read rule gets N_QUERY_FILTERED. This is
advice about possible filtering, never an assertion that overlapping roles
are disjoint or a change to query results.

### 13.2 Reviewed notes

`check --ack-notes` records all current Diagnostic notes in the shareable
`snapback/check-notes.json`, only after every ordinary check succeeds.
There is no implicit acknowledgement. Errors cannot be acknowledged.
The version-1 file stores a SHA-256 digest of the ordered `(filename, bytes)`
Quarry source set and reviewed diagnostic fingerprints (code, site, message,
rewrite and details). Any Quarry addition/removal/edit invalidates all entries;
a changed note also invalidates its fingerprint. A package version change alone
does not invalidate an unchanged finding. This deliberately conservative rule
makes rule/dependency changes require fresh review; no dependency inference is
claimed. Dynamic readiness/adoption notes must still match the whole finding.

Ordinary text prints only unreviewed notes, with a reviewed-count summary.
`--verbose` shows all notes for inspection. JSON always retains all findings in
`notes` and adds `newNotes` and `acknowledgedNotes` arrays, even with verbose;
automation never loses a finding. Invalid JSON or an unknown file format fails
check with a repair message, rather than hiding notes. Missing/stale entries
are unreviewed. Re-acknowledgement replaces the file with the current set.
Search certificates are separate from Diagnostic notes and remain visible.

### 13.3 Bounds and reference decision

E_CASCADE_BOUND names the enabling `delete <-` rule and its site, and offers
`delete <- deny` if that entry point is unintended; authored deletes and expiry
remain independent sources. `why` prints contextual reservation details while
ordinary check keeps arithmetic behind `--verbose`. E_QUARRY_FANOUT offers a
real product cap or resumable job, never paging an atomic mutation.
The writes guide runs mark → chunked child cleanup → parent deletion, and an
active-row guard using an ordinary required parent lookup plus a business
require, without modifying read-through semantics.

**Decision: do not introduce a `checked` reference spelling in 0.4.32.** The
proposed annotation conflates type identity, caller-readable lookup, physical
existence, and application-active status. A mere insert/update rule desugaring
would be bypassed by jobs/fixtures and would fail to define forward references,
keep/set-null actions or adoption. Calling that a checked reference would be a
false safety promise. The supported end state is an explicit physical
`insert <- ... and exists parents[.parent]` and corresponding update guard,
or a caller-readable mandatory lookup plus an active-state require when that
is the application invariant. These forms are enforced today and tested in the
packed guide recipe. References otherwise retain LLP 3000.039 §4.4. A future
annotation needs a separately reviewed whole-write/whole-lifetime constraint
contract, rather than a lint opt-out. This is a deliberate rejected alternative,
not an unimplemented claim of checked-reference support.

