   916	advice about possible filtering, never an assertion that overlapping roles
   917	are disjoint or a change to query results.
   918	
   919	### 13.2 Reviewed notes
   920	
   921	`check --ack-notes` records all current Diagnostic notes in the shareable
   922	`snapback/check-notes.json`, only after every ordinary check succeeds.
   923	There is no implicit acknowledgement. Errors cannot be acknowledged.
   924	The version-1 file stores a SHA-256 digest of the ordered `(filename, bytes)`
   925	Quarry source set and reviewed diagnostic fingerprints (code, site, message,
   926	rewrite and details). Any Quarry addition/removal/edit invalidates all entries;
   927	a changed note also invalidates its fingerprint. A package version change alone
   928	does not invalidate an unchanged finding. This deliberately conservative rule
   929	makes rule/dependency changes require fresh review; no dependency inference is
   930	claimed. Dynamic readiness/adoption notes must still match the whole finding.
   931	
   932	Ordinary text prints only unreviewed notes, with a reviewed-count summary.
   933	`--verbose` shows all notes for inspection. JSON always retains all findings in
   934	`notes` and adds `newNotes` and `acknowledgedNotes` arrays, even with verbose;
   935	automation never loses a finding. Invalid JSON or an unknown file format fails
   936	check with a repair message, rather than hiding notes. Missing/stale entries
   937	are unreviewed. Re-acknowledgement replaces the file with the current set.
   938	Search certificates are separate from Diagnostic notes and remain visible.
   939	
   940	### 13.3 Bounds and reference decision
   941	
   942	E_CASCADE_BOUND names the enabling `delete <-` rule and its site, and offers
   943	`delete <- deny` if that entry point is unintended; authored deletes and expiry
   944	remain independent sources. `why` prints contextual reservation details while
   945	ordinary check keeps arithmetic behind `--verbose`. E_QUARRY_FANOUT offers a
   946	real product cap or resumable job, never paging an atomic mutation.
   947	The writes guide runs mark → chunked child cleanup → parent deletion, and an
   948	active-row guard using an ordinary required parent lookup plus a business
   949	require, without modifying read-through semantics.
   950	
   951	**Decision: do not introduce a `checked` reference spelling in 0.4.32.** The
   952	proposed annotation conflates type identity, caller-readable lookup, physical
   953	existence, and application-active status. A mere insert/update rule desugaring
   954	would be bypassed by jobs/fixtures and would fail to define forward references,
   955	keep/set-null actions or adoption. Calling that a checked reference would be a
   956	false safety promise. The supported end state is an explicit physical
   957	`insert <- ... and exists parents[.parent]` and corresponding update guard,
   958	or a caller-readable mandatory lookup plus an active-state require when that
   959	is the application invariant. These forms are enforced today and tested in the
   960	packed guide recipe. References otherwise retain LLP 3000.039 §4.4. A future
   961	annotation needs a separately reviewed whole-write/whole-lifetime constraint
   962	contract, rather than a lint opt-out. This is a deliberate rejected alternative,
   963	not an unimplemented claim of checked-reference support.

