7772:| L11 | LLP 3000.007.001 goes to a **third** blind round as revision 3 — a private authoritative maintain plus a mirror — and that is the last round this session runs: READY from both builds nothing yet (§7's measurements gate the brief); anything less goes to the author with the residual list | Round 2 (GPT NOT READY, Grok READY WITH CHANGES) offered a structural simplification that dissolves three of its own Highs; the program's practice is three rounds, then descope or escalate |
7775:| L14 | **The horizon repair precedes maintained columns**, and gate 5 of LLP 3000.007.001 becomes the build lane's milestone zero (§21.10). The defect is filed P1 and handed to the wave 13 lead, who owns read completeness and is already in `engine/read.rs`'s horizon check; this session briefs the repair only if that lead declines it. **Resolved the same day: the wave 13 lead took it as lane W13g** — a correctness landing under §20.22's invariant, launched after W13c leaves `read.rs`, reviewed blind by two families, shipping with 0.2.29; this session stays out of `read.rs` and `device.rs`. W13g then **proved the boundary cannot be inferred from row images** (the stream serves current images, so a mutable ordering key can move below the floor before a device sees it; two histories with different floors produce byte-identical payloads) and the wave 13 lead authorised an additive snapshot field carrying each completed horizoned group's floor (lane W13g-2, the field under the same two-family review). **Landed on main 2026-09-17 from the reviewed tip `e4f274b479` after five blind rounds (§20.22.7); the acquisition's selection time is put to the author there.** | LLP 3000.007.001 §3.6 stated its rule before the measurement: past 10%, a device-side repair is designed and reviewed first. Measured: 58.3%. The alternative — refusing the column on horizoned tables — would refuse the flagship case and send builders back to the hand-kept form, which has the same defect and `update <- allow` besides. Gate 5 measures activation, which does not exist until it is built; a prototype measured first, with a stop, is the only honest reading of "before a lane is briefed" |
7780:| L19 | The wave 12 lead's ticket — a maintain value index cannot break ties by a group column — is taken into this program with a **recommendation: one rule, "a prefix of group fields, then `value`, then any remaining group fields, then `id`"**, as lane W15c after W15a and W15b, with a two-family blind review; *not launched*, because it changes the language surface and the author may prefer to wait for maintained columns, which need no maintain table for a ranking at all (§21.11) | Calibration #12's builder adopted the hour lanes and still kept a hand-written score, because a maintained ranking cannot be newest-first on ties. Group fields are fixed for a maintain row's life, so trailing ones cost key bytes and no extra index rewrite. Ordering ids by time (the ticket's option 2) would give meaning to an order the cards call opaque |
7785:| L24 | **Ruling 31's lane (W15b) is briefed and held, not launched**, although its one precondition (0.2.28 published) is met. It goes after the value-index tie-break (L19) or maintained columns, whichever the author chooses; this session launches no further lane | The generalised note's first rewrite is "keep the count in a maintain and read it". Calibration #12 showed a builder who did exactly that and still kept a hand-written score, because a maintained ranking cannot break ties newest-first (§21.11). A note whose advice the builder cannot follow is noise, and the census would be measuring false positives against a rewrite that is about to change. Everything else in the queue — the tie-break's language change, accepting LLP 3000.007.001, building it after wave 13's horizon repair — is the author's to start |
7787:| L26 | **Ruling 31's "derived in fact" is data dependence, not information flow.** A column is derived in fact when every value written to it is computed from reads (and ambient values) — an argument may *select* which read-derived value is written (a branch arm, a `± 1`, a sorted or sliced pick, how many rows a loop sums) without disqualifying it; only an argument that flows into the value itself (`score: amount`, `score + amount`, a field copied from an argument) does. Lane W15b's brief said the opposite ("any program argument flows in"), and two review rounds, consistent with that brief, silenced exactly the shape the ruling exists for: the like/unlike toggle on a hand-kept score (calibration 12's `posts.score`, calibration 10's `trendingRows.score`, the four calibrations' copies of our own recipe's `feedPullCounts.used`). The lane's next session implements this reading; the reviews judge it stated | The footgun P5 names is a count *kept by hand* under a rule that admits everyone: the caller cannot set it to 999, but can nudge it by any declared mutation, and the repair — a maintain, or the pinning rule — applies exactly. Under the information-flow reading the note fires only on the rarest form (a copied scalar) and is silent on the toughest (a toggle), which is the 0.2.26–0.2.28 builders' form three times over. Precision was bought at the cost of the purpose; a definition that names the purpose is the one to keep |
