## 12. Wave 11 cap recipe (QDIAG, 2026-10-14)

The resource law and runtime prices above are unchanged. `check` now computes
one recipe for the whole delete across its children, using the ordinary action
reservation plus the root write. Trial caps are evaluated together against all
seven ceilings, including rows and heap, rather than proposing 500 per child
and revealing the summed refusal later. The recipe reports rows/heap and names
each cap to apply. A maintained count source commonly reserves three writes
per child (the row and its old/new aggregate groups). Fixed transient and
retained-event heap matter as well. Existing smaller caps/unique bounds stay
smaller; a cap is always a product decision. Work earlier in the mutation uses
the same meter, so this is a reservation recipe, not a claim for arbitrary
preceding work. If no fixed fitting recipe exists, restrict and a job are the
repair. A maintained-sum source remains excluded from cascades; ordinary
system-job deletes unwind each contribution before the marked parent is deleted.

## 13. Wave 13: expiry orphan recovery

Implementer: Codex (DAY3), 2026-10-16. The 0.4.28 expiry sweeper physically
removed parents without applying declared reference actions. The 0.4.29 fix
applies actions only while a parent image still exists. A parent already swept
has no recoverable image or durable per-identity expiry marker in general:
unsynced tables have no partition event, and a commit's touched tables/counts
do not identify removed rows. Missing-parent references are also deliberately
legal (§4.4). Automatic adoption/sweep repair therefore cannot distinguish
historic expiry from valid intentionally dangling data. This amendment chooses
the commissioned explicit-recovery alternative rather than silently deleting
both classes. Adoption and scheduled sweeps preserve their existing semantics.
The 0.4.29 changelog is corrected to state this boundary.

Doctor reports E_EXPIRY_ORPHAN for physically missing identities in an ordinary
expiring parent table with writing reference actions. It uses the active stored
schema and one coherent read snapshot, scans child rows in bounded pages, and
returns at most 32 distinct identities per inspection. The finding says that
expiry provenance is unknown, including legitimate dangling references. Keep
references and missing non-expiring parents do not cause this finding. Inspection
writes nothing. A running temporary dev store is outside this persisted-store
inspection; doctor already labels it temporary.

After backup, review and stopping the host, the operator selects exactly one
missing root with `doctor --repair-expiry-orphan table:id` (and --release dir
for deployed storage). The existing owner lock excludes a running host. It
opens the active schema without adopting edited working source. The transaction
rechecks that the parent is physically absent and eligible, then invokes the
ordinary reference-action closure with an id-only root identity. It never
creates a parent row. Live children cascade or clear through current declared
actions, descendants use their own actions, restrict/read-through blockers refuse
the whole repair, constraints and maintained values apply normally, and ordinary
partition/change evidence commits atomically. Keep references remain unchanged.
Expired children retain ordinary closure semantics and can be swept later.
No application API or device behavior is added. A repeat without children writes
nothing; blocked/error paths commit no child changes. The operator chooses
whether the current schema is the intended recovery policy; the tool cannot
reconstruct the deleted parent's historical schema or image.

Verification includes a raw physical parent deletion simulating 0.4.28, reopen
and adoption/sweep nonrepair, read-only finding, cascade plus set-null plus keep,
nested cascade, restrict rollback, missing-root recheck, idempotent repair,
change evidence, and CLI owner-lock exclusion. Both commissioned blind reviews
cover this amendment and the DAY3 source/fixture/port contract before completion.

