snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:173:    ("N_REQUIRE_REFUSED", "a note, not an error — a branch of a mutation's `require` admits a caller by a column of the row it deletes (`person = viewer`, the member leaving) whom no disjunct of the table's delete rule admits: they pass the require and the delete refuses them E_RULE, never your code. Add the party to the delete rule, or drop the branch from the require. guide rules."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:174:    ("N_SEQUENCE_READ", "the first member cannot authorize its own sequence allocation through an existing-membership read rule. A newly created parent does not prove a fresh shared counter. Preserve the roster audience with the manual allocator in guide sequences, or deliberately choose owner-only read authority. check names the sequence declaration and read rule."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:179:    ("E_QUARRY_MAINTAIN", "aggregate view predicates use row-local typed scalar comparisons; group keys are columns such as .author, admitted one-hop paths, or day/hour/bucket of required time columns or recorded times. Calendar offsets are nonnegative literals smaller than the period. Scalar reads need the complete key. Follow the expression-specific rewrite; guide maintains."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:180:    ("E_QUARRY_MAINTAIN_EXPIRE", "stored top-N can miss a live winner before the physical sweep. A server count upper-bound threshold scan would be sound but is not built and would have to refuse on devices, whose snapshots deliver live values. Signed sums have no such upper bound. For a window over an entity's immutable time, use the feeds card's Trending predicate with both time endpoints and a maintained column over a non-expiring source. For expiring contributions, read live totals without ordering by stored .value. guide maintains."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:200:    ("E_ADOPT", "the compiled schema cannot adopt the stored rows. check reads the dev store (or --store path) without changing it; dev refuses the same successor step. Add an optional column or a literal default, or repair existing values/duplicate keys before narrowing. now and viewer belong to inserts and cannot fill old rows. guide types."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:267:    ("E_BOUND", "family bound — the meter cut a mutation (or a response exceeded 16 MiB): the message names the meter, the ceiling and the site, never the count (it would measure rows the viewer may not read). Expired rows not yet swept never count toward the examined ceiling. For delivery reports, E_BOUND is an ordinary non-retryable refusal: the SDK calls onDeliveryRefused once and parks the interval until the next start, which retries with a new id. There is no retry within the session. A committed report that runs again is a no-op report (guide receipts). E_BOUND is not marked retryable and carries no backlog count. For other bound causes, read a smaller page, narrow its where predicate, or move the sweep into a job."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:309:    ("E_QUARRY_SYNTAX", "the parser expected the token or construct named at file:line:column. Object fields use name: expression (or a bare name for name: name); ...value spreads a present row or object; ...shape inside a row/page shape names a declared shape. Operations need name(args): followed by an indented body, including () for no arguments. Close each (, [ and { with its matching delimiter; separate fields and arguments with commas. The rewrite names the expected spelling; `check --fix` applies only repairs marked mechanical. Read `snapback4 guide language` for complete examples. `check` hides a parser-proven empty-operation cascade after an earlier error in that operation; `--verbose` and `--json` retain it. Independent errors stay visible. A query merge lane takes a prefix page, range(a, b), or a bounded list; walk is not a lane domain. A time function occurs at most once per index."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:313:    ("E_QUARRY_TYPE", "a value meets a declaration of another type; the message names both. A computed index key needs a required time column of its table, a valid literal period/offset, and no real column with the same function name; see guide indexes. Computed keys are not writable columns."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:314:    ("E_QUARRY_AMBIGUOUS", "a positional key matches more than one declared unique key. Name the columns, for example profiles[principal = viewer]; for a population use a where predicate and column order such as by .at. Older explicit-index reads can still name their index. In min/max, a comma after by can belong to the read order: write min((receipts first 20 by .id), .state), or bind page first and write min(page, .state). If a min/max row expression can also bind an enclosing shape row, move the aggregate into a helper and pass enclosing values by name. If .field can name both a collection column and an enclosing collection value, bind the enclosing value to a local for a binary call, or move the aggregate out of the enclosing shape. guide indexes."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:318:    ("N_IMMUTABLE_PATCH", "on readable targets, an immutable patch succeeds only when its value equals the stored value; omit it if another value may update this row. guide writes."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:322:    ("E_SYNC", "a sync line cannot be honoured, or a membership insert or update rule could grant its audience without the caller already holding it; the message names the rule term, column or index, and the rewrite prints the edit against your tables. A rule with several readers syncs as a union when each `or` branch is one whole audience (`.column = viewer`, or at most one `exists group[.columns, viewer]`); otherwise it can stay as written under `online only` (queries answer from the server; mutations never predict and queue offline). Unions, a roster, owner-managed teams and open groups: guide sharing. To insert the first synced roster member, use `created teams[.team]` after inserting that parent in the same mutation; guide sharing --section \"A team the owner manages\" shows the self-bootstrap. Copied audiences and mine/team/public libraries: guide rule-recipes."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:324:    ("N_SYNC_ORDER", "a note, not an error — a device-capable query pages a windowed table on another index (including a calendar index) or oldest first. Whole-domain aggregates and word indexes have separate coverage requirements and are outside this ordering advice. Its acquisition boundary proves only newest-first pages on the window's own index, within that boundary. The note names both orders and the source site. A paste-ready page uses last N on the window index, audience equalities in bracket keys, and the original predicates and cursor. It requires a cursor, a cap covering the whole group, or the same ordered columns already read newest first. A cursorless partial page in another order gets words because reordering could select different rows. Restart pagination when changing order; the full traversal keeps the same rows. Only scope-independent range endpoints can move into residual filters. Outer-row endpoints, generated membership lanes, explicit merges and projected views get the window order and index in words without a paste-ready replacement; one lane is not the whole read. Capped pages get last N from their bound scan. Otherwise the ordinary query needs the server; offline coverage is unknown. A held query stays on the device and reports incomplete coverage without server fallback. online only currently applies to tables and views, not query headers. guide sync."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:337:    ("E_SEARCH_OPEN", "membership search cannot finish discovery or opening within one read. The refusal names the authorized membership count (a lower bound if discovery is unfinished), the meter envelope and the site. Use the grouped form in guide search; no multi-request search-work session is implemented."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:339:    ("N_SEARCH_OPEN", "check's search certificate: membership discovery and every authorized head must fit one read on each page. Past that envelope E_SEARCH_OPEN names the count and rewrite to the grouped search_all form."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:340:    ("N_SEARCH_WORDS", "check's search certificate: inner first-term word expansion and further-term misses remain metered, including repeated expansion on a compact membership refill. Use a longer first term or search a pinned chat. guide search."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:342:    ("N_WIDE_MERGE", "a note, not an error — a merge visits every source lane. Uniform plain lanes without setup and with a non-nullable unique index or one ending in id read one head each, then advance the winning lanes; other lanes read a page each. Discovery, rule probes, lookahead, duplicates and expired rows still consume the meter. Inspect structural costs with `snapback4 check --cost`; if a held query answers this read, delete the merge. Relationship-driven merges may benefit from entries per reader (guide feeds). `why` opens the first matching diagnostic's card in the current project: feeds for Following, search for membership search; without a matching diagnostic it defaults to search. Membership search points to guide search, section 'Search every chat I'm in': the grouped one-cursor form continues through a meter cut; the newest form is one globally ordered stream while every head opens in one read. An opening or word-limit cut has no merge continuation. A resumable read-meter cut while an owning uniform lazy merge drains established heads can carry `next` after its last fully shaped emitted row (§5.6), subject to final-answer eligibility, with `complete: false, cut: 'meter'`. Heap exhaustion clears the whole page even during the drain; step exhaustion aborts and output exhaustion can refuse after materialisation. A terminal full page need not carry `next`; following a cut does not clear `usePage`'s accumulated incompleteness."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:344:    ("N_NO_CURSOR", "a note, not an error — a returned page without a cursor cannot continue. If the screen pages, declare `c: cursor ?` and page with `after c`, preserving its predicate, direction and order. Filling first N or last N is complete without next. Composed fields omit the note, as do pages covering a group cap, or a pinned unique key's rows, that fits the examined ceiling. Meter cuts, undecided read-rule prefixes and missing device coverage still make answers incomplete. guide reads."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:347:    ("N_SPARSE_FILTER", "a residual predicate may examine many rejected rows before finding a page. Pin its scope with where .owner = owner. For a time window, give both endpoints, for example .at > now - 24h and .at <= now. The engine chooses the index; the read meter still bounds work. guide reads."),
snapback4/crates/snapback4-lang/src/guide/codes.rs:350:    ("N_NEVER_COMPLETE", "a note, not an error — a page whose read rule reads a column the scan does not pin is complete: false on the server whatever it finds. Pin that column with a where equality. For membership search, use messages matching q where exists members[.roomId, viewer] last 20 by .at after c; or read held rows on the device. guide reads, search."),
