he recipient cap using only parent/expiry index keys. The compiler exports complete per-source `source_reservations`, calculated by the same functions the server uses, including ordinary private maintains in schemas without receipts. A device skipping receipt-derived or online-only public aggregate work pays the shared server worst-case reservation, including on insert; an ordinary server insert may charge less actual work. Receipt allocation pays a fixed 51-key allowance; successful inserts also pay for the server counter replacement without writing or reading a guessed counter on the device. Receipt-derived sums and counter scopes with additional data-dependent hooks make their writers server-only. Ordinary online-only aggregates keep predicting within their fixed reservation. Ordinary synced aggregates still execute their shared delta path. Private formulas and totals stay off the device. Every predicting source write also charges declared image/video list slots, even for null or empty cells, without taking the asset ledger: per slot, 2 rows written, 4 examined, 8 steps and 16,384 transient heap bytes. The implicit result of a final insert/update is the pending write image, not an authored whole-row read. Use a stable message id to predict an edit. Allocation has one runtime refusal guard: after touching provisional allocation, any error rolls back the overlay and keeps the intent queued. Receipt schemas without compiler classification also queue. Field names are static; arbitrary forged compiler metadata is outside the backend contract. No per-value taint or count-path traversal runs. A kept backend without the new capabilities opens with only affected programs downgraded: receipt-source writes, hidden effects, existing receipt readers and writes whose complete price cannot be recovered queue. Unrelated fully priced posts or conversation-title edits still predict offline under Kept. An old private-column projection without prices cannot identify hidden source effects, so every unpriced table writer conservatively queues, including unrelated profile saves and posts. This affects both Clique calibration apps. Supplying the bundled backend while offline does not replace Kept; prediction resumes at the first online adoption of that backend. With no private-column owner markers anywhere, the structural zero proof keeps unrelated zero-cost writers predicting. Outbox ids, arguments and delivery debt survive; see `guide offline-interactions` for the upgrade note. Replaying a write id allocates nothing twice. Reports assert the recipient's
